A||N||3||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Iron Duke:  If the lifters all seem perfect, is the cam good too?||||5||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||06-21-2001||03:08 AM||||HI,<P>My freshly rebuilt Iron Duke has a ticking noise in the valve train at 1000 miles.  <P>I suspected that the new Compu Cam camshaft and lifters did not break in properly, so I have removed the rocker cover and valve cover to inspect the lifters (a lot easier that pulling the cam).<P>I looked at each lifter and they all look the same on the surface that contacts the camshaft, nice and shiny.  You can see that they have been spining as intended, but otherwise they are like mirrors.<P>If the lifters all look this good, can the camshaft still be "lobe-impaired"?<P>The engine also will not rev past about 4800 rpms.  It is like hitting a wall, no more acceleration.  It feels aweful, but the engine makes great power from idle up to this limit.  There is however some roughness at all rpms.<P>Before the rebuild, the old duke with failing main bearings would rev to 5,000 without the same "hitting a wall" feeling higher despite the stock cam.  <P>The compression in my rebuilt Duke is 160 psi in all four cylinders + or - 2 psi (best I can tell with this gauge).  The engine ticks hardest at 2000 rpm.  <P>I expected to find at least two scored lifters as the noise has a hard tick and a lighter tick in the background. <P>How loud is the tick?  With the decklid closed you have to know what to listen for (unless it's at 2,000 rpm then it's not hard to hear).  With your head over the engine it's easiest to hear from the top or underneath and in front of the passenger rear wheel.  <P>All of the valve springs and rockers appear to be in good shape (new also).  The head was rebuilt with new exhaust valve guide inserts.  <P>I did re-use the old pushrods from a donor engine that did not make any ticking noise.<P>What next?  Should I pull the camshaft and inspect it directly?||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||06-21-2001||05:14 PM||||Bad camshaft lobes don't make ticking noises. Collapsed lifters and exhaust manifold leaks do. Sounds to me like your camshaft is in perfect order. Your engine hits a wall @ 4800 rpm because your computer has a rev limiter. It shuts down the fuel or spark to limit the RPM.  <P>I'm not sure about your engine but the later dukes have non-adjustable positive stop rocker arm bolts, which means they could be just in need of an adjustment. Sometimes the valves get held off their seats when an engine with positive stop rocker bolts gets put back together with a resurfaced head. You may need to convert this engine to an adjustable rocker arm like the v6 and small block Chevy or get custom length pushrods.<P>Check to make sure you don't have an exhaust leak. People often mistake it for a lifter noise.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||06-21-2001||10:09 PM||||There is a crack in my engine block between the pushrods for #2 and #3 cylinders.  It is not hard to miss!<P>It looks like my rebuilt engine failed.  The block was tested for cracks prior to machining, so I guess that theory is out the window.<P>The block was decked to flat (&lt;0.010") and overbored 0.030".  The camshaft was the second mildest one I could find.  This setup was far from radical.<P>I guess that the production 84 Iron Duke block is weaker than expected.  Maybe I could find another and start over, but it just seems like gambling to me.<P>Is it time for Super Duty?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000003||88formula||06-21-2001||10:14 PM||||OUCH! Sorry to here that. Maybe you could try JB Weld. That’s not a joke. That stuff has been used for engine block cracks before. ||||reg||1
